## Deployment

### Cloudflare Workers

The repo is set up for [`@opennextjs/cloudflare`](https://opennext.js.org/cloudflare).
Connect the GitHub repo once in the dashboard — **Workers & Pages → Create → Import a
repository** — and every push to `main` builds and deploys itself.

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Build command | `npm run cf:build` |
| Deploy command | `npx wrangler deploy` |
| Version command | *(leave empty)* |

Everything else comes from [wrangler.jsonc](wrangler.jsonc), including the
`nodejs_compat` flag and the compatibility date, both of which the adapter requires.

To try it locally before pushing:

```bash
npm run cf:preview   # builds and serves the real Worker on localhost
npm run cf:deploy    # builds and deploys from your machine
```

If you set `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL`, add it under **Settings → Variables** as a *build*
variable — it is read at build time, not at runtime.

### Notes on how it is wired

Two things were changed so this runs on Workers, both of which are fine everywhere else:

- **Locale redirects moved out of middleware.** Next 16 pins proxy files to the Node.js
  runtime and the Cloudflare adapter only runs Edge middleware, so `src/proxy.ts` is gone
  and `redirects()` in [next.config.ts](next.config.ts) does the job from the routing
  manifest instead.
- **`dynamicParams` on the project route is back to its default.** Pinning it to `false`
  made every detail page 404 on Workers, which resolves those routes through the server
  function rather than as static files. Unknown slugs still 404 correctly, via
  `notFound()` in the page.

### Vercel

Still works unchanged: import the repo and set `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL`. The Cloudflare
files are inert there.

